```
=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router_core_1
diagnosis_text:
The physical (L2) interface router_core_1:eth1 — the sole direct link between
router_core_1 and router_core_3 (172.16.0.2/31 ↔ 172.16.0.3/31) — is in a
hard DOWN state. The kernel shows the interface with flags <BROADCAST,MULTICAST>
only, missing both UP and LOWER_UP, meaning there is no carrier detected. FRR
on router_core_1 reflects this immediately: "eth1 is down / OSPF not enabled on
this interface," so no OSPF Hellos are being sent or received on that segment.
As a result, router_core_1 has no OSPF neighbour entry for router_core_3 at all.
router_core_3's eth0 is fully operational (acting as DR, Dead Timer still
counting), but it will expire the stale adjacency entry as soon as the Dead
interval elapses — it is already seeing zero inbound Hellos from router_core_1.

Traffic is not dropped because OSPF has converged over the alternate path
router_core_1 → router_core_2 → router_core_3; the NOC report of no user
impact is consistent with this rerouting. However, the backbone is now
single-homed for pod 1's path to the data center, as the ticket correctly
identifies.

Root cause: router_core_1:eth1 lost its link (carrier-down event), taking the
direct core_1–core_3 OSPF adjacency with it.

Corrective fix: Restore the link on router_core_1:eth1. If the interface was
administratively shut down, bring it back with `ip link set eth1 up` (inside
the container or via FRR `no shutdown`). If the underlying virtual cable/bridge
was disconnected at the Kathara/Docker level, reconnect it. Once carrier is
restored, FRR will re-enable OSPF on eth1 and the adjacency with router_core_3
will re-form automatically within one Hello interval (10 s).
=== END ===
```